<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saladin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1193">1193</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Salah al-Din Yusuf ibn Ayyub, Salah al-Din · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saladin (Salah al-Din Yusuf ibn Ayyub) was a Kurdish Muslim military leader and the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>, who died in 1193. He became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1169, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> and united Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> under his rule. His victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> in 1187 and recapture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> made him a celebrated figure, though accounts of the Ayyubid Empire note that his division of territory among relatives prepared the empire's fragmentation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="Saladin" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group (CNG) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_(Saladin)._AH_564-589_(1169-1193_CE)_%C3%86_Dirham_(30.1mm,_13.28_g,_6h)._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_(AD_1190-91)._Sultan_sitting_facing,_cross-legged,_on_high-backed_throne_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Saladin</b>'s nephew, Sultan al-Kamil, later handed Jerusalem to Emperor Frederick II by treaty in 1229, in return for help against his own Ayyubid relatives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Al-Azhar, developed as a teaching institution from 988 onwards, became the most important centre of Islamic learning in the Arab world, a status it retains today although <b>Saladin</b> reversed its Ismaili orientation after 1171.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom won important victories, including King Baldwin IV’s defeat of <b>Saladin</b> at the Battle of Montgisard in 1177, and King Amalric I invaded Egypt several times between 1163 and 1169, briefly forcing it to pay tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Relations with Egypt worsened under the Ayyubids: after a Nubian attack on Aswan, Shams al-Dawla Turan-Shah, brother of <b>Saladin</b>, sacked Qasr Ibrim in 1172–1173.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Baldwin I of Jerusalem and Sigurd I of Norway took it after a siege and made it the centre of the Lordship of Sidon; <b>Saladin</b> captured it in 1187, German crusaders restored it in 1197, the Ayyubids destroyed it in 1249 and the Mongols under Kitbuqa in 1260.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Saladin</b> took Amid from the Zengid coalition in April 1183, the Ayyubids counted among its stores 80,000 candles, a tower full of arrowheads and 1,040,000 books.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tikrit Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.6,43.69&amp;year=1183" aria-label="Tikrit Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5660 911 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Saladin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6110 1181h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6110 1181h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.6,43.69&amp;year=1183">What was here in 1183? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
